.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt BSDSYSCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDSysctl
.Nd system.Sysctl capability provider
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the provider for the
.Dq system.Sysctl
capability.
A client holds a system.Sysctl channel and asks
.Nm
to read, and \(em policy permitting \(em write, kernel
.Xr sysctl 3
variables by name, instead of calling
.Xr sysctl 3
itself or forking a Casper
.Xr cap_sysctl 3
helper.
.Pp
.Nm
is a socket-free
.Xr libservice 3
launched on demand by
.Xr switchboard 8 ;
it ships as the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Sysctl.cap
bundle and is not spawned from
.Pa /usr/sbin .
Up to 128 client channels are multiplexed in the single provider process.
Each ready channel receives a bounded dispatch batch, so an idle client does
not prevent other clients from making requests.
A session without a complete request for 30 seconds is closed; write readiness
alone does not renew that deadline.
Each authenticated label may hold at most 16 sessions.
Additional sessions are closed when either limit is reached.
Sessions speak the FIDL library
.Sy bsd.sysctl.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/sysctlcmp.fidl ;
see
.Xr fidl 7 .
A session must first negotiate the wire version; a method before
negotiation, an unknown method, a malformed frame, a pre-FIDL record or an
attached descriptor closes that session without a reply.
The gate remains in its original process:
.Nm
holds a
.Dq system
gate token that is close-on-fork, so a
.Xr pdfork 2
worker could not inherit it.
After the per-label policy check, a Get or Set of a capability-mode-confined
node is performed
.Em through
that gate token in kernel context;
.Xr __sysctlbyname 2
in capability mode reaches
.Dv CTLFLAG_CAPRD Ns / Ns Dv CAPWR
nodes directly, so the raw syscall stays confined and the sandbox is never
loosened.
No Casper helper is involved.
.Sh POLICY CONFIGURATION
Access is scoped per label by
.Pa Config/sysctl.conf
in the bundle (UCL), delivered to
.Nm
as a directory descriptor.
The
.Va default
block applies to any label without a
.Va clients
entry.
.Va read
and
.Va write
are lists of sysctl name prefixes (dotted-path, matched on a component
boundary): a name is permitted if it equals or lies under a listed prefix.
Writes are denied unless listed.
A read of an unlisted name, or any unlisted write, is refused with
.Er EPERM .
A missing or malformed file falls back to the compiled-in default (a small
safe read set, no writes) with a warning.
.Sh KERNEL ISOLATION
The bundle's
.Va isolate
list controls which privileged sysctl writes the kernel protects from
ordinary UNIX callers, including root, while a covering claim remains active.
The shipped list contains only
.Va kern.maxfiles .
Scoped isolation matches exact numeric OIDs, not name prefixes.
Unlisted sysctls retain their ordinary kernel permissions.
Reads and nodes marked
.Dv CTLFLAG_ANYBODY
are outside this write gate; this preserves name resolution and self-scoped
process-title updates.
.Pp
A write covered by an active claim requires the owning nonzero capability
nonce or explicit authorization from a covering owner.
Root privilege alone does not bypass that check.
The per-label read/write policy described above is a separate boundary:
it controls what clients may ask this provider to perform.
An ACL entry does not add an OID to the kernel isolation list.
.Pp
Isolation lasts only while a covering claim remains active.
Closing the last covering claim releases this additional protection;
ordinary non-capability callers then return to normal UNIX checks.
Provider restart and claim ownership must therefore be included in lifecycle
testing; isolation is not a persistent boot-time deny list.
A legacy coarse claim protects all privileged sysctl writes for its lifetime.
.Sh INTERFACE INVENTORY
The fixed interface-inventory operation reads
.Dv NET_RT_IFLISTL
through the held sysctl token and returns at most 256 interface summaries.
The caller must have read permission for
.Dq net.routetable .
No permission is added to the shipped default policy.
The explicit
.Va system.Network/bsdnetwork
client entry grants only read access to
.Va net.routetable
for network state observation; it grants no writes.
No caller-supplied MIB or write data is accepted.
Native records are validated and reduced to names, indexes, flags, MTUs,
and link states; addresses and counters are not returned.
The temporary native dump is capped at four megabytes, with three attempts
when the interface list grows during collection.
See
.Xr sysctlcmp_interfaces 3
for the snapshot and error contracts.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libsysctlcmp 3 ,
.Xr sysctl 3 ,
.Xr fidl 7 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
